> API and interpretation

Reference keys and messages are public deterministic fixtures. Production keys
require a secure random source and must stay secret. The32 words and two keys
show actual sensitivity and collisions, not empirical uniformity or resistance
to an adaptive adversary. No runtime-specific default-hash claims are needed.
The legacy hash signature keeps arbitrary-length support; checked run/trace APIs
default to a one-million-byte policy.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Aumasson and Bernstein (2012), SipHash: a fast short-input PRF|https://www.aumasson.jp/siphash/siphash.pdf]
- [Reference implementation and official vectors|https://github.com/veorq/SipHash]

